  :adr/body "# ADR 2799008143: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 8143 Paper Products Machine Operators plant scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 8143\n-- Paper Products Machine Operators** only. This is Wave3 (production/\ntrades); Wave0/1/2 are 100% complete, the entire 75xx craft-trades\nrange is complete, and the initial 81xx mining/plant range is\ncomplete; this actor continues the 81xx/82xx plant-and-machine-operator\nrange (`kotoba.occupation.wave/wave-of \"8143\"` resolves to wave 3).\n\n`gh api repos/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8143` returned a clean\n404 before any work began -- this was a genuinely fresh scaffold, not a\nresume of prior partial work. `gh api repos/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8142`\nalso 404'd (the closest sibling reference had not yet landed), so this\nactor mirrors the next-closest landed reference instead (see below).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"8143\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"8143\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Paper Products Machine Operators\"\n-- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's\n`:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics\n:identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`. This ADR and\nits companion registry edit promote the `8143` entry to `:implemented`.\n\n**Industrial paper-converting machine hazard dimension.** Paper\nProducts Machine Operators run converting/cutting/folding equipment\n(box-making, bag-making, tissue-converting machines) -- a real\nheavy-machinery hazard domain: crush, entanglement and cutting-blade\ninjury from rollers and blades. This actor does NOT model direct\nconverting-equipment operation authority or plant-safety-clearance\nauthority. It is deliberately restricted to PLANT SCHEDULING and\nLOGISTICS COORDINATION around the occupation (production-run/\ninventory/progress-record logging, crew/shift scheduling, safety-\nconcern escalation, and raw-paper/converting-materials procurement\ncoordination) -- never the machine-operation-execution decision or the\nplant-safety-clearance decision itself, and never an override of a\nplant safety officer's judgment. The closed proposal-op allowlist\nstructurally excludes any op that could directly finalize such a\ndecision, and the governor independently, permanently hard-blocks any\nproposal whose rationale text names such a finalization action,\nregardless of the declared `:op` or confidence -- this is not an\nescalation path a human can approve through, it is an outright,\nstructural refusal at every maturity level.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8122`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8122)\n(Metal Finishing, Plating and Coating Machine Operators -- a closely\nrelated industrial converting-plant safety pattern), independently\nre-cloned and read in full this session (the intended `8142` sibling\nreference had not yet landed and 404'd cleanly, so this ADR falls back\nto the next-closest already-landed reference per the task's fallback\ninstruction). Its `platingcoord.{store,advisor,governor,actor}` module\nsplit, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph shape, closed op-allowlist and\nscope-exclusion self-trip-guard design translate almost 1:1 onto this\noccupation's own heavy-machinery-hazard-adjacent coordination scope,\nso this ADR's `papercoord.*` modules follow the same shape with domain\nterms substituted (plater -> converter, plating facility/plant ->\npaper-converting facility/plant, plating-operation-execution ->\nmachine-operation-execution, chemical-safety-clearance ->\nplant-safety-clearance).\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8143` as an OSS\ndocumentation/logistics-coordination actor for paper products machine\noperators, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every\nprior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Paper Products\nPlant Scheduling Coordination Advisor ⊣ PaperCoordGovernor** on a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`,\ngit-sha-pinned identically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- a\ndifferent library coordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the\nunrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **plant scheduling/logistics coordination\nONLY, never converting-equipment operation authority** -- it must\nNEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose\nexercising a machine-operation-execution decision or a\nplant-safety-clearance decision.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- production-run/inventory/progress data\n  logging only\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/shift/task scheduling proposal\n  only\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a machinery-hazard/blade-condition/\n  equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates immediately, NEVER\n  auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- raw-paper/converting-materials\n  procurement proposal only\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase,\nresembles a machine-operation-execution finalization decision or a\nplant-safety-clearance finalization decision -- these are structurally\nabsent from the op-allowlist entirely, not merely gated behind\nescalation.\n\n### Five HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Converter unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered converter (crew member) record\n   (`:no-converter`). Never trusts the proposal's own claim of who the\n   converter is.\n2. **Facility unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered paper-converting facility/line record\n   (`:no-facility`).\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (`:no-actuation`; the governor never dispatches hardware\n   itself and never operates converting equipment itself).\n4. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op\n   allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`).\n5. **Scope-excluded action** -- the proposal's `:op` or `:rationale`\n   must never name a machine-operation-execution finalization, a\n   plant-safety-clearance finalization, or a plant-safety-officer-\n   judgment override (`:scope-excluded-action`), REGARDLESS of the\n   declared `:op` or confidence. This is a hard, PERMANENT block --\n   there is no maturity level or confidence at which this actor may\n   auto-commit or human-approve its way past this rule; it is an\n   outright refusal, not an escalation.\n\n### Escalation gates (always human sign-off)\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's always-escalate path). The only channel by\n  which the robot may surface a roller-entanglement/blade-injury/\n  crush-hazard/equipment-condition observation -- it never resolves\n  the concern itself.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000) -- always needs human sign-off,\n  independent of confidence (boundary verified as exclusive by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`: exactly-at-threshold\n  commits, over-threshold escalates).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, CLAUDE.md)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as bare NOUNS (e.g.\n\"paper\", \"blade\", \"roller\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- and this vertical's domain\nvocabulary is an especially sharp case of that risk, because routine\nwork-log/task text legitimately names converting machinery by name\n(e.g. a task literally described as \"routine converting task with\npaper rollers and cutting blades\" -- equipment identification, not a\nproposal to finalize a converting operation or clear the plant as\nsafe). `papercoord.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased\nexclusively as finalization ACTION PHRASES (\"proceed with the\nconverting run\", \"finalize the converting operation\", \"declare the\nplant safety cleared\", \"override the plant safety officer's\njudgment\"), never bare nouns, and is checked only against the\nproposal's `:rationale` field (the advisor's own generated text),\nnever against raw `:task` input text.\n`papercoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-\ntrip-on-scope-exclusion` is a dedicated regression test driving the\ndefault mock-advisor across all four allowed ops with a task field\ncontaining exactly those bare equipment nouns, asserting none of them\never hard-trip the finalization-scope rule. Green on first run.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"8143\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8143` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-8143`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary` immediately before the edit,\n  not assumed, to account for concurrent sibling landings in this same\n  6-agent batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 30 tests, 65 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's `:test/\n  counts` and independently re-verified against a brand-new fresh\n  clone of the pushed repository (not just the local working copy). No\n  mojibake in the Japanese violation-detail strings (0\n  replacement-byte instances grepped in the fresh clone).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely\n  via the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent\n  with sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`) -- a single-entity EDN vector with an\n  `:adr/body` string blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-converting-run`/`:approve-\n  plant-safety-clearance` that is merely gated behind an\n  always-escalate path: rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  guidance -- an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to\n  finalize a converting operation or a plant-safety clearance exists\n  in the code path and is merely deferred to a human gate. For a\n  decision whose failure mode is crush/entanglement/cutting-blade\n  injury, the capability itself must not exist in this actor at all.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"paper\" \"blade\"\n  \"roller\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above -- this domain's own vocabulary necessarily\n  discusses converting machinery by name to do its logging/scheduling\n  job, so a bare-noun list would self-block the actor's own legitimate\n  proposals whenever a task description mentions rollers or blades.\n- Naming the crew-member field `:operator-id` (matching the ISCO title\n  \"...Machine Operators\" literally): rejected -- `docs/operator-\n  guide.md` and this fleet's convention already use \"operator\" to mean\n  the deploying business/entity running the actor, so reusing the same\n  word for the individual crew member would create a confusing\n  collision; `:converter-id` (mirroring the reference's `:plater-id`\n  pattern, and the paper industry's own term \"converting\" for\n  cutting/folding/box/bag/tissue processes) keeps the two senses\n  distinct.\n\n## Test verification\n\nFresh local run immediately before pushing:\n\n```text\nRunning tests in #{\"test\"}\n\nTesting papercoord.actor-test\n\nTesting papercoord.governor-test\n\nRan 30 tests containing 65 assertions.\n0 failures, 0 errors.\n```\n"}]
